{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    # Implements a simplified bisection method to find a zero within a fixed range.\n    low, high = -10.0, 10.0  # Initial search range for a zero.\n    tolerance = 1e-6         # Desired precision for the zero.\n    max_iterations = 100     # Maximum iterations to prevent infinite loops.\n\n    # Handle degree 1 (linear) polynomial explicitly for directness.\n    if len(xs) == 2:\n        a, b = xs[0], xs[1]\n        if abs(b) > tolerance: # Avoid division by zero for constant polynomials.\n            return -a / b\n\n    # For higher degree odd polynomials, a zero is guaranteed to exist.\n    # Use bisection within the initial range, assuming it contains a root.\n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        mid = (low + high) / 2\n        f_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(f_mid) < tolerance:\n            return mid\n\n        f_low = poly(xs, low)\n\n        if f_low * f_mid < 0:\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            low = mid\n\n    return (low + high) / 2 # Returns the midpoint after max iterations."}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "def find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    # Internal parameters for bisection method, derived from design document\n    tolerance = 1e-6\n    max_iterations = 1000\n\n    # Initial search interval.\n    # Start with a small interval and expand until a sign change is found.\n    # Since the polynomial has an even number of coefficients, its degree (len(xs) - 1) is odd.\n    # An odd-degree polynomial always has at least one real root and its values\n    # approach -infinity and +infinity at the ends, guaranteeing a sign change exists.\n    a, b = -1.0, 1.0\n\n    # Try to find an interval [a, b] where poly(a) and poly(b) have opposite signs.\n    # The loop should not exceed max_iterations to prevent excessive search.\n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        fa = poly(xs, a)\n        fb = poly(xs, b)\n\n        if fa * fb < 0:\n            break # Found a bracket\n\n        # If one of the endpoints is a root (within tolerance)\n        if abs(fa) < tolerance:\n            return a\n        if abs(fb) < tolerance:\n            return b\n\n        # Expand the interval by doubling the bounds\n        a *= 2\n        b *= 2\n\n        # Prevent numerical overflow for extremely large bounds\n        if abs(a) > 1e15 or abs(b) > 1e15:\n            raise ValueError(\"Bounds became too large during interval expansion.\")\n\n    # If after searching, no interval that brackets a zero is found.\n    # This scenario should ideally not be reached for odd-degree polynomials given the problem constraints.\n    if fa * fb > 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Failed to find an initial interval that brackets a zero.\")\n\n    # Bisection method for finding the root within the bracketed interval\n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        mid_point = (a + b) / 2.0\n        f_mid = poly(xs, mid_point)\n\n        if abs(f_mid) < tolerance:\n            return mid_point\n\n        if fa * f_mid < 0:  # Root is in the lower half [a, mid_point]\n            b = mid_point\n            fb = f_mid\n        else:  # Root is in the upper half [mid_point, b]\n            a = mid_point\n            fa = f_mid\n\n    # If the loop finishes without finding a zero within tolerance\n    raise ValueError(\"Zero not found within maximum iterations.\")"}
